<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ezo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yezo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ezo was the Japanese name for the lands north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a>, especially the island now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a>. From the 16th century the Matsumae clan controlled its southern tip and the Ainu trade, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> took direct control at times of Russian pressure. In 1869, after the short-lived Republic of Ezo, it was renamed Hokkaido and opened to settlement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ezo.jpg" alt="Ezo" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alain Manneson Mallet (1603-1706) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mallet-TerredeGesso.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ezo, also written Yezo or Yeso, covered the people and lands to the north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>: the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a> and at times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Sakhalin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuril-islands">Kuril Islands</a> as well. Ezogashima, the Island of the Ezo, was divided into districts. The Wajinchi, or Japanese Lands, covered the Japanese settlements on and around the Oshima Peninsula.</p><p>The rest was the Ezochi, the Ezo-land or Ainu Lands, though Japanese people from elsewhere also moved to its coasts. The Ezochi was in turn divided into three: North Ezochi, covering southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Sakhalin</a>; West Ezochi, the northern half of Hokkaidō; and East Ezochi, the populous south and east of Hokkaidō together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuril-islands">Kuril Islands</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Japanese sources that give an etymology describe the name as probably a borrowing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a> word enciw, meaning person or people. It is first attested in Japanese in a text of 1153, for any of the non-Japanese peoples of north-eastern Honshū, and in 1485 for the northern islands where they lived. The kanji were chosen for their meaning rather than their sound: shrimp or lobster, and barbarian. The first may allude, through the long antennae of those animals, to the prominent beards of Ainu men.</p><p>Read as Emishi, the same two characters name the people of the region, whose descendants are most likely related to the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a>. The spelling Yezo reflects the pronunciation of about 1600, when Europeans first came into contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and survives in scientific <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> names such as Fragaria yezoensis and Porphyra yezoensis.</p><p>The first published description of Ezo in the West was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by Isaac Titsingh in 1796. His small library of Japanese books included Hayashi Shihei's Sangoku Tsūran Zusetsu, An Illustrated Description of Three Countries, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1785. In 1832 the Oriental Translation Fund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and Ireland supported an abridged edition of Titsingh's French translation, published after his death and completed by Julius Klaproth after the first editor, Jean-Pierre Abel-Rémusat, had died.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ezo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
